-- title:
--   Gauss-order function at a supersingular node: slope drops
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, a nonzero level $N$, an algebraically closed perfect field $k$ of characteristic $q$, a ring homomorphism $\mathrm{red}\colon A\to k$, modular polynomial data for $q$ satisfying the Kronecker congruence, integrality of the two degeneracy embeddings from level $N$ to level $Nq$, a place specialisation $P$ and a prolongation tuple $R$ over $P$. Assume $q\nmid N$, that $R$ is a model (its two divisor laws and two cusp laws), that it satisfies `OrderLawFixed`, and fix a finite set $W_0$ of places of the level-$N$ function field over $k$, each supersingular (rational, affine geometric, with supersingular $j$-value), for which $R$ satisfies the regularity law and the node value law. Fix intermediate fields $K\le K'$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q$ with $K'/\mathbb Q$ finite, a place $w\in W_0$ with $\varphi\cdot w\in W_0$ for $\varphi=$ `arithFrobC q k N` (the semilinear automorphism induced by the Frobenius of $k$), the value-integrality law at $w$, and the inclusion of the ring of $w$-node integers over $K$ into that over $K'$. Fix $\varpi$ in $A\cap K'$ whose multiples are exactly the elements killed by $\mathrm{red}$, an integer $e_{K'}\ge 1$ and a unit $\varepsilon$ with $q=\varpi^{e_{K'}}\varepsilon$ in $A\cap K'$. Fix node coordinates $c=(x,y)$ over $K$ at $w$ (so $x$ reduces to $0$ along the first prolongation and has order $1$ at $\varphi\cdot w$ along the second, and symmetrically for $y$), an integer $e_w\ge 1$ and a unit $u$ of the node ring over $K'$ with the node equation $xy=\varpi^{e_we_{K'}}u$ there. Assume the ideal generated by $\varpi,x,y$ in the node ring over $K'$ is maximal and is its only maximal ideal, that this ring is local and Noetherian with that ideal as maximal ideal, that the ideals generated by $(\varpi,x)$ and by $(\varpi,y)$ are prime with $y$ outside the first and $x$ outside the second, and that every element of the ring differs from the image of some constant in $A\cap K'$ by a non-unit. Let $\mathrm{depth}$ be a natural-number-valued function on the places of the level-$Nq$ function field over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ satisfying the depth value law for $c$: for every place $V$ with $P.\mathrm{reduceFst}\,V=w$ that is fixed by the arithmetic Galois action of the inertia subgroup of $A$ over $\mathbb Q$, the $A$-valuation of $V(y)$ equals $v_A(q)^{\mathrm{depth}\,V}$. Finally let $f\neq 0$ lie in the level-$Nq$ function field, with its Laurent expansion in the subfield generated by the constants of $K'$ and the two $j$-series, assume that subfield is the field of fractions of the node ring over $K'$ at $w$ in the explicit sense that every element becomes a quotient of two of its elements, and let $c_1,c_2\in K'$ be nonzero with $c_1f$ in the integers of the first prolongation and nonzero residue there, $c_2f$ in the integers of the second prolongation and nonzero residue there. Let $D$ be the divisor $V\mapsto \operatorname{ord}_V f$, assume every $V$ in its support with $P.\mathrm{reduceFst}\,V=w$ is fixed by the inertia action, and assume the node total equality $\sum_{V:\,P.\mathrm{reduceFst}\,V=w}D(V)=\operatorname{ord}_w(\overline{c_1f}^{(1)})+\operatorname{ord}_{\varphi\cdot w}(\overline{c_2f}^{(2)})$. The conclusion asserts the existence of $G\colon\mathbb N\to\mathbb Z$ with $(G(d)-G(d-1))-(G(d+1)-G(d))=\sum_{V:\,P.\mathrm{reduceFst}\,V=w,\ \mathrm{depth}\,V=d}D(V)$ for all $d$ with $1\le d<e_w$, with $G(1)-G(0)=\operatorname{ord}_w(\overline{c_1f}^{(1)})$, $G(e_w)-G(e_w-1)=-\operatorname{ord}_{\varphi\cdot w}(\overline{c_2f}^{(2)})$, and $v_A(c_1/c_2)=v_A(q)^{G(e_w)-G(0)}$.
--
--   This is the Gauss-order (Newton-polygon) bookkeeping for a nonzero function $f$ on the annulus attached to a supersingular node of the special fibre at $q$ of the curve of level $Nq$: under the node-total equality, the sampled Gauss orders form a function whose interior slope drops count the zeros and poles of $f$ by depth along the annulus, whose end slopes are the orders of the reductions on the two branches, and whose total slope records the ratio of the two normalising constants. It is used in the construction of the component charts and attached annuli for a model, and in the computation of the component-group projection.
